{"version":2,"width":110,"height":54,"timestamp":1789631644,"idle_time_limit":2.0,"command":"bash tmp/co2-demo.sh","title":"token-finops: 08-savings-co2","env":{"SHELL":"/bin/zsh"}}
[0.007711, "o", "\u001b[1;32m$ \u001b[0mtoken-finops savings --co2 --cloud-region us-virginia\r\n"]
[0.100529, "o", "Local inference: Mac Studio M4 Max, 128 GB + Qwen3 32B (Q4)\r\n  throughput:        25 tok/s  ->  11.11 h per 1M tokens\r\n  energy:            90 W load  ->  1.00 Wh per 1k tok, 1.00 kWh per 1M tok\r\n  tariff:            Germany household (BDEW, Aug 2026) = 0.370 EUR/kWh\r\n  energy cost:       $0.40 / 1M tok\r\n  capex:             $3,999 over 3 y at 20% utilisation = $0.761/h -> $8.45 / 1M tok\r\n  local total:       $8.86 / 1M tok\r\n\r\nCloud comparison (sonnet-class, 15% output tokens): $3.20 / 1M tok  (Claude Sonnet 5)\r\n  -> local is 2.77x the cloud price: CLOUD CHEAPER\r\n  -> break-even utilisation: 60% of 24/7 (14.5 h/day of inference)\r\n\r\nGreen IT: gCO2e per 1M tokens (energy only -- capex/embodied emissions are NOT in either figure)\r\n  local:  ~   344 g   [computed]  1.00 kWh/1M tok x 344 g/kWh\r\n                      Germany household (BDEW, Aug 2026)\r\n  cloud:  ~   183 g   [ESTIMATE]  600 Wh/1M tok accelerator-side x PUE 1.13 x 270 g/kWh\r\n                      Northern Virginia 'Data Center Alley' (eGRID SRVC) -- "]
[0.100538, "o", "CLEANER than the US average; PUE 1.13 (hyperscale fleet average; Google 1.09, AWS 1.14, Microsoft 1.17)\r\n  -> local emits ~1.9x the cloud estimate per token\r\n  !! The cloud figure is an ORDER-OF-MAGNITUDE ESTIMATE, not a measurement. No cloud AI provider\r\n     publishes energy per token. Basis: Epoch AI's ~0.3 Wh/query at its own stated 500 output tokens/query, x datacentre PUE, x published grid carbon intensity.\r\n     Confidence: LOW -- the grid and PUE inputs are solid, but the energy-per-token input is a derived estimate good to roughly a factor of 2-3 upward. It is a magnitude, not a measurement.\r\n     Grid figure: EPA eGRID2023 subregion SRVC ~270 g/kWh; EIA state profile for Virginia (data year 2024) gives 286 g/kWh -- https://www.eia.gov/electricity/state/virginia/ . Driven by ~28-40% nuclear and only ~1.9% coal. CAVEAT: Virginia imports ~30% of the electricity it consumes from the dirtier western PJM pool (eGRID RFCW, ~416 g/kWh), so a consumption-based figure would be materially higher than this gene"]
[0.100544, "o", "ration-based one.\r\n     Every source, and why the error bars are this wide: docs/CO2_ESTIMATE.md\r\n\r\nCaveats: throughput/power figures are editable defaults (see hardware_profiles.json, reviewed 2026-09-15); quality equivalence is an assumption; a sonnet-class local model does not replace Opus/Fable-class work."]
[0.100558, "o", "\r\n"]
[0.105111, "o", "\r\n"]
[1.612805, "o", "\u001b[1;32m$ \u001b[0mtoken-finops savings --co2 --power solar-de-feed-in --cloud-region us-ercot | tail -n 8\r\n"]
[1.680121, "o", "  -> local emits ~0.18x the cloud estimate per token (local is cleaner)\r\n  !! The cloud figure is an ORDER-OF-MAGNITUDE ESTIMATE, not a measurement. No cloud AI provider\r\n     publishes energy per token. Basis: Epoch AI's ~0.3 Wh/query at its own stated 500 output tokens/query, x datacentre PUE, x published grid carbon intensity.\r\n     Confidence: LOW -- the grid and PUE inputs are solid, but the energy-per-token input is a derived estimate good to roughly a factor of 2-3 upward. It is a magnitude, not a measurement.\r\n     Grid figure: EPA eGRID2023 subregion ERCT ~334 g/kWh; EIA state profile for Texas (data year 2024) gives 373 g/kWh -- https://www.eia.gov/electricity/state/texas/ . Gas supplies ~50% of generation, but wind+solar supply ~28.5%, which is why a 'gas-heavy' grid still lands at or below the national average.\r\n     Every source, and why the error bars are this wide: docs/CO2_ESTIMATE.md\r\n\r\nCaveats: throughput/power figures are editable defaults (see hardware_profiles.json, reviewed 2026-09-15); "]
[1.680127, "o", "quality equivalence is an assumption; a sonnet-class local model does not replace Opus/Fable-class work.\r\n"]
[1.680428, "o", "\r\n"]
[3.190191, "x", "0"]
